In this research, we investigate policy improvement techniques for Markov Decision Processes with certain constraints, where the state space is divided into distinct subgroups. We focus on developing enhanced policies that maintain a high probability of adherence to these constraints. By leveraging concentration inequalities, we establish that our enhancement methods converge rapidly, providing an upper bound on increased constraint violation under our approximations. We then apply these results to a problem involving a multi-armed bandit with constraints, introducing a new algorithm, $\text{CARE}$. On a multi-armed bandit with constraints, $\text{CARE}$ performs better in practice than other algorithms, such as UCB-epsilon and Thompson Sampling, often outperforming them significantly. Furthermore, our algorithm, subject to certain conditions on the reward function, achieves a regret bound of $\tilde{O}(\sqrt{T})$. This makes CARE the first policy-enhancing algorithm to achieve such an $\tilde{O}(\sqrt{T})$ regret bound for constrained MDPs. Additionally, our analysis reveals that a previously established regret bound for a similar algorithm, $\text{BEAR}$, is not entirely accurate and does not hold under all conditions, yet it still achieves a faster rate of convergence in practice. We also extend our research to constrained finite MDPs, where the results show that our algorithm, $\text{BEAR}$, can be enhanced to match the performance of the optimal policy. By combining $\text{BEAR}$ with a new value function approximation, $\text{BEAR-VP}$ based on linear programming, we establish that both $\text{BEAR}$ and $\text{BEAR-VP}$ converge to the optimal value function at a geometric rate. This research provides a new perspective on improving policies for constrained MDPs and MCMDPs, offering a novel approach to policy enhancement. The code for our algorithm is available online. https://github.com/ucb-ailab/constrained-bear-v2.0. Additionally, our study of the pseudo-co