This research investigates the effect of using pre-trained neural features from WavLM, a pre-trained model for speaker recognition, on tasks involving overlapped dialogue and speaker gender identification in dialogue. Specifically, we examine the impact of WavLM's output layer, its attention encoder, and layer-wise pre-training loss on these tasks. Our findings indicate that using the speaker embedding output significantly outperforms other options in overlapped dialogue recognition. In the context of gender recognition, we observe that using the speaker embedding output and pre-training loss with diagonal matrices yields the best results. Notably, we also find that using the speaker embedding output outperforms other methods in overlapped dialogue recognition. To our knowledge, our best overlapped dialogue recognition model achieves an unprecedented performance. Our study contributes to the existing understanding of how different components of pre-trained models affect specific tasks. The code used in this research will be made available. Additionally, we provide access to our experiment results, which will facilitate further research and comparison with other research approaches. 

